<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karma Phuntsok Namgyal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1597">1597</a>–<a href="/years/1632">1632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karma Phuntsok Namgyal was a king of the Tsangpa dynasty, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse-dzong">Samdrubtse</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a>). In 1618 he conquered Ü, ending the last influence of the Phagmodrupa dynasty and bringing most of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> under Tsangpa rule. He patronised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu">Karma Kagyu</a> and opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school and its Mongol allies.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His family background is somewhat unclear. A religious biography makes him the son of Karma Thutob Namgyal, ruler of Upper Tsang, and a lady from Yargyab; other sources make him the son of the rulers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhingshak-tseten-dorje">Karma Tseten</a> or Karma Tensung. The law code issued by his son Karma Tenkyong says vaguely that Karma Thutob Namgyal and his brothers had him as their son, which suggests the Tibetan practice of polyandry. Some texts date the death of his predecessor Karma Tensung to 1611.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>At his accession he held most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a>, Toh in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and parts of Ü. In 1612 and 1613 he subjugated Mangyül Gungthang in Ngari, as well as Latö Lho and Latö Chang, then turned swiftly east and defeated the Phagmodrupa king Mipham Wanggyur Gyalpo in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-valley">Yarlung Valley</a>. Lopa, Dagpo, Phanyul and Neu came under his rule, though these dependencies were far from secure.</p><p>Offended when the entourage of the Fourth Dalai Lama, Yonten Gyatso, refused him an audience, he called the governors and hierarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse-dzong">Samdrubtse</a> in December 1614 and accepted the Black Hat Karmapa, Choying Dorje, as desi, while real power stayed with the Tsangpa. He refused to recognise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Ngawang Namgyal</a> as the reincarnation of the Drukpa scholar Kunkhyen Pema Karpo and resolved to have him killed; Ngawang Namgyal fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> in 1616, and a Tsangpa force sent there under Lagunas was defeated.</p><p>He built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmapa">Karmapa</a> monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a> named Tashi Zilnon, the suppressor of Tashilhunpo, after the nearby Gelugpa house. In 1618, when Chokhur Mongols on pilgrimage raided Tsangpa cattle, he invaded Ü. Monks of Drepung and Sera who resisted suffered great losses, and several Gelugpa monasteries were forced to convert. Choying Dorje appointed him ruler of Ü and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a>, and he became known as cakravartin, world ruler, though he never overcame his enemies completely. Mangyül Gungthang was finally incorporated in 1619.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When his physicians could not cure an illness, the Panchen Lama, Lobsang Chökyi Gyaltsen, was supposedly called to treat him, and in reward he lifted the ban on searching for the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>. The boy was found in 1619 and installed as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a>, Ngawang Lobsang Gyatso. The king died soon after; one account says a tantric spell cast by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Ngawang Namgyal</a> brought death to him and his two wives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>. His son Karma Tenkyong was the last king of the Tsangpa dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karma Phuntsok Namgyal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His descendants expanded steadily, and in 1618 <b>Karma Phuntsok Namgyal</b> conquered Ü, ending the last Phagmodrupa dynasty influence and ruling most of central Tibet.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsangpa-dynasty">Tsangpa dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
